  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Ratified in 1967 following the JFK assassination, the 25th Amendment establishes clear procedures for presidential succession, vice-presidential vacancies, and handling presidential incapacity. It ensures orderly transfers of power if a president dies, resigns, is removed, or is unable to discharge duties. Key aspects include: Section 1 (Succession): If the President is removed, dies, or resigns, the Vice President becomes President. Section 2 (VP Vacancy): The President nominates a new Vice President, confirmed by a majority vote of both Houses of Congress. Section 3 (Voluntary Transfer): The President declares in writing an inability to serve, making the Vice President Acting President until a written declaration to the contrary is submitted. Section 4 (Involuntary Transfer): The Vice President and a majority of the Cabinet can declare the President incapacitated, immediately making the VP Acting President. If the President disputes this, Congress decides the issue. Historical Usage and Cases: 1973: Gerald Ford was nominated as Vice President by Richard Nixon after Spiro Agnew resigned. 1974: Gerald Ford became President following Richard Nixon's resignation. 1974: Nelson Rockefeller was appointed Vice President by Ford. Temporary Transfers: Used by Presidents Reagan (1985), G.W. Bush (2002, 2007), and Biden (2021) for medical procedures requiring anesthesia. Section 4 has never been used. The amendment is designed to provide stability, ensuring a functioning executive branch in crisis situations.",
